  "scenes": [
    {
      "narration": "Take one thousand units of money. Depending on the country, that same number buys three completely different lives.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "hook",
          "art": "globe",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ],
          "lines": [
            "1,000 units.",
            "Three worlds."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s1"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A thousand US dollars buys you roughly a month of groceries. A thousand Japanese yen buys about one lunch. A thousand units of a collapsed currency buys almost nothing.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "rows",
          "kicker": "What 1,000 units buys",
          "rows": [
            {
              "rank": "$",
              "name": "1,000 USD",
              "value": "≈ a month",
              "note": "of groceries",
              "color": "#1f7a3d",
              "bar": 1.0
            },
            {
              "rank": "¥",
              "name": "1,000 JPY",
              "value": "≈ 1 lunch",
              "note": "about $7",
              "color": "#17120f",
              "bar": 0.25
            },
            {
              "rank": "Z",
              "name": "1,000 old ZWD",
              "value": "≈ nothing",
              "note": "hyperinflated away",
              "color": "#c0392b",
              "bar": 0.04
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "narration": "Economists line these up using something called purchasing power parity.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-ppp",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · purchasing power"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "narration": "And if you push a currency far enough, like Zimbabwe did, the money essentially dies.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-zim",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · Zimbabwean dollar"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "narration": "The dollar keeps its value largely because the world keeps it in reserve.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-reserve",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · reserve currency"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "narration": "The lesson is that the number printed on the note is almost meaningless. What matters is what it actually buys.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "truth",
          "art": "priceTag",
          "line": "It's not the number",
          "sub": "it's the trust behind it"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "So what is a thousand units of your money really worth where you live?",
